  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nTonight through next Tuesday...Overall, quiet weather will continue \nto reside across the forecast area during much of this forecast \nperiod. A cold front will propagate east through the region \novernight into early Thursday morning. This could squeeze out a few \nshowers during this time. However due to a moisture-starved air mass \nover the region, the majority of the forecast area will remain dry \nas it moves through.\n\nBehind this front on Thursday, noticeably colder drier air will \nadvect into the region.  Dew points through the day will gradually \nfall into the upper 30s to mid 40s. Meanwhile, highs will range from \nthe middle 60s north to middle 70s south.  With afternoon relative \nhumidity values generally ranging between 30-40%, northwest winds \nsustained around 15 mph with gusts around 25 mph, coupled with the \nextremely dry soil conditions and vegetation across the area, a \n\"limited\" threat for fire danger will exist across the entire CWA.\n\nColder drier weather will exist Friday through the weekend and into \nthe beginning of next week.  High temperatures Friday through Monday \nwill generally range from the middle 50s to the low and middle 60s, \nbut increasing into the upper 50s to middle 60s come Tuesday. Lows \non the other hand will be quite chilly during this time frame. \nCurrently, the coldest temperatures during this stretch look to fall \nboth Saturday morning and Sunday morning with lows advertised to \nrange from the upper 20s to middle 30s, particularly across the \nnortheast half of the forecast area where temperatures are more \nlikely to range from the upper 20s to lower 30s.\n\nLastly, a disturbance will move east across the northern Gulf of \nAmerica late in the weekend into the first part of the new work \nweek.  This could result in some showers across the CWA during this \ntimeframe. /19/",
